<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abbey of Saint Martial, Limoges</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/848">848</a>–<a href="/years/1791">1791</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Abbey of Saint Martial was a Benedictine monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limoges">Limoges</a>, founded in 848 around the tomb of Saint Martial, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of the city. From the tenth to the twelfth century it was among the great abbeys of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, known for its library and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of early polyphonic music; the chronicler Adémar of Chabannes worked there. The abbey was suppressed and demolished after the Revolution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abbey-of-saint-martial-limoges.jpg" alt="Abbey of Saint Martial, Limoges" width="320" height="350" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean Fayen et Maurice Bouguereau · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint-Martial_sur_plan_de_Limoges.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The abbey grew from a cemetery outside the Roman settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limoges">Augustoritum</a>, on the site of the Place de la République at the commercial heart of Limoges. The cemetery was held to be the burial place of early Christian martyrs, among them Saint Martial, and became a place of pilgrimage in Merovingian times. By the 6th century, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a>, a funerary chapel stood over Martial's tomb in the care of a small community of clerics.</p><p>These clerics were recognised as a congregation of canons under the Carolingian ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> (814–840), and the community became a Benedictine abbey in 848 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>. A mosaic above the tomb, set in hard cement of an earlier period, shows that the shrine was already well established when the abbey was founded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The abbey grew alongside the City of the Castle, a commercial centre under the abbot's patronage and outside the control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s Cathedral City. Late in the 9th century the saint's body was placed in a golden shrine in a great new church, which drew pilgrims on the Way of St. James. The abbey was at its height in the century after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cluny-abbey">Cluny Abbey</a> took it over in 1065, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a>, also Duke of Aquitaine, stole the shrine.</p><p>A century of rebuilding and renewed prosperity followed, then the destruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> and the conflicts of the 16th and 17th centuries, from which the abbey never recovered. The community was dissolved in 1791; demolition began the next year and was complete by 1807, and the site became the Place de la République. Excavations begun in 1960 uncovered the crypt with the tombs of Saint Martial and Saint Valerie of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limoges">Limoges</a>, opened to the public from 1966.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The abbey was a centre of liturgical chant, early polyphony and troubadour song. Early in the 11th century Roger de Chabannes brought in his nephew Adémar as cantor and scriptor of notation. Adémar composed sequences, prosulae and music for a festal octave of the patron, and tried in vain to have Martial recognised as an apostle. Among the earliest sequences written at the abbey is the Swan Sequence of about 850.</p><p>Its school and library gathered an almost complete repertoire of West Frankish tropes and sequences, as well as the two-part settings known as Aquitanian polyphony. Some of the earliest troubadour lyrics with their melodies survived in its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, which are now in the Bibliothèque Nationale.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abbey of Saint Martial, Limoges” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Most people were peasants growing grain and vines under lay and ecclesiastical lords, and the region was dense with monasteries such as <b>Saint-Martial of Limoges</b>, Saint-Hilaire of Poitiers and Saint-Jean-d'Angély.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
